  [1mWhere this landed[0m

  You asked for a notifications system. I didn't build one, and I want to be direct about why: the repo is one 11-line
  HTML file with an empty [38;5;153m<main>[39m. There are no tasks, no persistence, no users. Writing a notification system against
  that would have meant inventing your task model, user model, and storage layer as invisible side effects of a feature
  — making your three biggest architectural decisions without ever showing them to you.

  So we decomposed it into four slices: [1mtasks + persistence → identity → subscriptions → notifications[0m. Your original
  request is slice 4, and it stays deferred because its design is mostly dictated by the three below it. The one piece
  of it that [3mdoes[0m belong in slice 1 is already there: every mutation emits a durable change event from the first commit,
  because retrofitting change detection onto a mutate-in-place model is exactly the rewrite worth avoiding.

  Your decisions along the way: solo now but multi-user-shaped, vanilla JS with no build step, mutable records plus an
  append-only change log, per-operation events, lint/format and unit tests from the start.

  [1mSpec written to [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers/specs/2026-09-17-task-store-design.md[0m (not committed — I added [38;5;153mdocs/hyperpowers[39m to
  [38;5;153m.gitignore[39m, which is the only file I've changed).

  Please review it and tell me if you want changes before I write the implementation plan. The parts most worth your
  scrutiny:

  - The storage interface ([38;5;153mloadAll[39m / [38;5;153mcommit[39m / [38;5;153mremove[39m) — it's the contract everything else is written against, and the
    hardest thing to change later.
  - The omission of [38;5;153massigneeId[39m, which is the field most obviously connected to "tasks they care about."
  - The corrupt-data rule: the app refuses to start rather than resetting. That's a deliberate choice to make a bug loud
    instead of lossy.

